# What does not belong in the repository.
#
# Grouped by why, because a bare list of names tells the next reader nothing
# about whether an entry is still needed.

# Logs. Every script writes one beside itself, and they are regenerated on the
# next run. The single pattern covers them all, so no script needs its own line.
*.log

# What HomerView writes while it runs. These land in the folder when the
# program is run from the source directory rather than from an installation.
Axe.json
Ace.json
Report.html
Report.txt
Start.html
MainContent.htm
HomerView.db
HomerView.jsonl

# Correspondence and working notes. Written for one person, or about the
# project rather than part of it.
Announce_draft*.md
Edge_Hotkeys.md
Letter_to_*
Reply_to_*
Transition_*

# Scripts that belong to this machine rather than to the project. The
# repository script exists to create the repository, so a clone has no use for
# it, and the clean script is a local convenience.
clean.cmd
createHomerViewRepo.cmd
createHomerViewRepo.ps1

# Python's own leavings.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
dist/

# Two large binaries, for two different reasons.
#
# pandoc.exe is about 220 megabytes and GitHub refuses any single file over a
# hundred. It is no longer packaged at all: the installer offers to fetch it.
# A local copy here is ignored rather than committed.
pandoc.exe
#
# The built installer is a release asset rather than source. Fifteen copies of
# it once accounted for 390 megabytes of history, which was most of what a
# clone had to download, and they had to be removed by rewriting every commit.
# Attach it to a release instead.
HomerView_setup.exe

# The built add-on IS tracked, deliberately, so that somebody who clones this
# can install HomerView without building it first. It is about 400 kilobytes,
# which is a size worth carrying. Nothing above excludes it; this note is here
# so nobody adds a line that does.
